---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import katex from "katex";
import { board6 } from "../lib/board6";

const k = (tex: string) => katex.renderToString(tex, { throwOnError: true });
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.endsWith("/")
  ? import.meta.env.BASE_URL
  : `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/`;

// the rule in three pictures: the plus, an arm forcing, the last arm winning
const plusPicture = board6({
  black: [
    [2, 2],
    [3, 2],
  ],
  labels: [
    [1, 2, "a"],
    [2, 1, "a"],
    [2, 3, "a"],
    [1, 1, "d"],
    [1, 3, "d"],
    [3, 1, "d"],
    [3, 3, "d"],
  ],
});
const armPicture = board6({
  black: [
    [2, 2],
    [3, 2],
    [2, 1],
  ],
  white: [
    [0, 0],
    [4, 4],
  ],
  threats: [[3, 1]],
  squares: [[2, 1, 1]],
});
const lastArmPicture = board6({
  black: [
    [2, 2],
    [3, 2],
    [2, 1],
    [1, 2],
    [2, 3],
  ],
  white: [
    [0, 0],
    [4, 4],
    [3, 1],
    [1, 1],
  ],
  threats: [
    [1, 3],
    [3, 3],
  ],
});
---

<Base
  title="The Square Achievement Game on n × n Boards"
  description="Two players claim cells of an n by n grid; the first to own four corners of a square wins. The first player wins exactly when n is at least 5, by a rule with no lookup table from 6 by 6 up, machine-checked in Lean. Play against it."
>
  <header class="masthead">
    <h1>The Square Achievement Game on n × n Boards</h1>
    <p class="byline">
      Sean Reid · October 2026 ·
      <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22410">arXiv:2610.22410</a>
    </p>
  </header>

  <p class="lead">
    Two players take turns claiming cells of an
    <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> grid. The first to hold all four
    corners of an axis-parallel square wins; a full board with no square is a
    draw. Erickson asked who wins. The first player wins exactly when
    <span set:html={k("n \\ge 5")} />, and from 6 × 6 up a rule of one paragraph
    wins, with no lookup table. The whole argument, every board size at once, is
    checked in Lean 4.
  </p>

  <div class="theorem">
    <span class="label">Theorem.</span> In the square achievement game on the
    <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> board, the first player has a winning
    strategy if and only if <span set:html={k("n \\ge 5")} />. For
    <span set:html={k("n \\le 4")} /> the game is drawn under optimal play: each
    player can secure at least a draw. For every
    <span set:html={k("n \\ge 6")} />, the first player following the plus rule
    wins.
  </div>

  <p>The rule, for every board from 6 × 6 up:</p>
  <ol class="rule">
    <li>
      Open at (2,2). Answer the first reply at (3,2), except against seven first
      replies near the opening, where a small diagram in the paper gives the
      second move.
    </li>
    <li>
      If some empty cell completes a square of the first player, play it.
      Otherwise, if some empty cell completes a square of the second player,
      block it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Otherwise follow the line chosen after the second reply: at most three
      tempo moves, single forcing moves each answered by a forced block, and
      then the arms of a plus.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <figure class="diagram rule-pictures">
    <svg
      viewBox="-4 -4 480 152"
      role="img"
      aria-label="Three 6 by 6 boards: the plus of side 1 at (2,2) with its arms and diagonals; an arm played, threatening the diagonal between it and the held arm; the last arm played, threatening two diagonals at once"
    >
      <defs>
        <pattern
          id="grid6p"
          width="24"
          height="24"
          patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse"
        >
          <path
            d="M24 0H0V24"
            fill="none"
            stroke="var(--rule)"
            stroke-width="0.8"
            opacity="0.5"
          ></path>
        </pattern>
      </defs>
      <g set:html={plusPicture} />
      <g transform="translate(164 0)" set:html={armPicture} />
      <g transform="translate(328 0)" set:html={lastArmPicture} />
    </svg>
    <figcaption>
      The plus, in three pictures. Left, the first player holds the centre (2,2)
      and one arm (3,2); the other arms are a, the diagonals d. Middle, playing
      an arm perpendicular to the held one makes three corners of a small
      square, so the second player must block on the diagonal, dashed. Right,
      after two such arms, the last arm threatens two diagonals at once, and no
      single block stops both. When the second player holds an arm or a
      diagonal, the rule first plays up to three tempo moves, each answered by a
      forced block, to place the stones it needs.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    The rule reads only its own stones, the second player's stones inside the 17
    × 17 base board, and one derived cell, the hidden corner, so one rule serves
    every board, and it plays the same 79 lines on every board from 6 × 6 to 17
    × 17. Against a second player who always blocks every line ends by ply 17;
    that bound is computed from the lines and is not part of the formal theorem.
    On 5 × 5 no purely forcing strategy from (2,2) exists, and a table with
    quiet moves at branch points plays instead.
  </p>

  <figure class="game">
    <sag-board n="6"></sag-board>
    <figcaption>
      Play against the rule. The bot moves first, explains each move, and draws
      the line it is following; click a cell, or use the arrow keys and Enter.
      Change n to explore: on 5 × 5 the table plays, and below 5 × 5 neither
      side can force a win.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <nav class="routes" aria-label="where to go next">
    <a class="route" href={`${base}downloads/paper.pdf`}>
      <span class="route-title">Read the paper</span>
      <span class="route-body">
        The outcome theorem, the rule in full, why plus arms force, the
        verification runs, the simulation theorem that carries one checked board
        to all larger ones, and the formal theorem that the rule's own play
        wins. PDF, built from this repository; also on arXiv as 2610.22410.
      </span>
    </a>
    <a class="route" href={`${base}proof/`}>
      <span class="route-title">See how the proof works</span>
      <span class="route-body">
        An illustrated guide that follows the paper section by section: pluses,
        the hidden corner, tempo moves and lines, the small boards and the 5 × 5
        branch point, the forced checker and its guards, and the simulation
        theorem that carries one run on the 23 × 23 anchor board to every larger
        board.
      </span>
    </a>
    <a
      class="route"
      href={`${base}downloads/square-achievement-formalization.tar.gz`}
    >
      <span class="route-title">Check it yourself</span>
      <span class="route-body">
        The Lean 4 development. The rule is <code>plusMove</code>, a definition
        of 250 lines with no data; a successful <code>lake build</code> is the
        proof, and the README shows how to print the theorems and their axioms.
      </span>
    </a>
  </nav>

  <h2>What you have to trust</h2>
  <p>
    The headline statements in the development read as follows, with
    <code>P1CanWin</code> the first player forcing a win, the two draw
    predicates each player securing at least a draw, and{" "}
    <code>WinsWith σ n h</code> the tree in which the first player plays the
    move of the strategy <code>σ</code> at every node after the history{" "}
    <code>h</code>, against every legal reply, ending in a first-player square
    on every branch.
  </p>
  <pre class="lean"><code>theorem outcome (n : ℕ) :
    (P1CanWin n ∅ ∅ ↔ 5 ≤ n) ∧
      ((P1CanDraw n ∅ ∅ ∧ P2CanDraw n ∅ ∅) ↔ n ≤ 4)

theorem plus_wins_with {"{"}n : ℕ{"}"} (h : 6 ≤ n) :
    WinsWith plusMove n []</code></pre>
  <p>
    The definitions behind them, cells, squares, the winning predicate as a
    strategy tree, and the 250 lines of <code>plusMove</code>, are the only part
    a skeptic has to read. Beyond them the trust is Lean's kernel, mathlib, and
    <code>native_decide</code>, which lets the Lean compiler evaluate the
    exhaustive checks instead of the kernel. The 5 × 5 table was found by a
    Python search, but nothing about that search enters the proof, since a wrong
    table fails the check. The bot on this page is a TypeScript copy of the
    rule. The site's tests replay it against the lines the Python implementation
    commits to on every reply pair of the 6 × 6 board and on the anchored
    positions of the proof, and the paper reports that those lines agree with
    the Lean definition.
  </p>

  <h2>Open questions</h2>
  <p>
    The rule wins by ply 17, a bound computed from its lines. How quickly can
    the first player force a win on the
    <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> board, and can one rule of comparable
    length be fastest for every <span set:html={k("n")} />? The rule's second
    move has seven exceptions; is there an opening that needs none? The 6 × 6
    region and the 17 × 17 base board are least in the senses the paper makes
    precise, but whether a rule can read less than the second player's stones
    inside the base board is open. And 5 × 5, where no purely forcing strategy
    from (2,2) exists, still has no short rule.
  </p>
</Base>
